<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 -->
<section id="s-kvcache" data-code="kvcache">
<div class="section-no">01 — The KV Cache Problem</div>
<h2>Why Transformers Can't Scale to Long Contexts on Edge</h2>

<p>
  Every transformer-based LLM carries a silent memory cost that compounds with every token you feed it.
  For each new token in the prompt, the model must store a <strong>key</strong> and <strong>value</strong> vector
  for every layer — the KV cache. This cache is how attention "remembers" past tokens without re-computing them.
</p>

<p>
  The problem: KV cache memory is strictly <strong>O(n)</strong> in sequence length. Double the context, double the RAM.
  For Llama 3.2 3B with 28 layers, 8 KV heads, and head dim 128, the formula is exact:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">KV Cache Size Formula</div>
  KV&nbsp;bytes&nbsp;=&nbsp;2&nbsp;×&nbsp;<span class="math-var">L</span>&nbsp;×&nbsp;<span class="math-var">n_layers</span>&nbsp;×&nbsp;<span class="math-var">n_kv_heads</span>&nbsp;×&nbsp;<span class="math-var">head_dim</span>&nbsp;×&nbsp;dtype_bytes<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— factor 2: one K tensor + one V tensor per layer</span><br><br>
  Llama&nbsp;3.2&nbsp;3B&nbsp;@&nbsp;32k&nbsp;tokens:&nbsp;2&nbsp;×&nbsp;32768&nbsp;×&nbsp;28&nbsp;×&nbsp;8&nbsp;×&nbsp;128&nbsp;×&nbsp;2&nbsp;≈&nbsp;<strong>3.6&nbsp;GB</strong><br>
  <span class="math-comment">— for KV cache alone, before weights (6 GB) or activations</span><br>
  LFM-3B&nbsp;@&nbsp;32k&nbsp;tokens:&nbsp;fixed&nbsp;recurrent&nbsp;state&nbsp;≈&nbsp;<strong>~2&nbsp;MB</strong><br>
  <span class="math-comment">— constant regardless of sequence length</span>
</div>

<p>
  At 32k context, Llama 3.2 3B needs 3.6 GB just for key/value tensors — far exceeding the RAM budget of any mobile phone.
  This is why "128k context window" claims for 3B transformers are largely theoretical: models physically cannot
  store that context on the device they're supposed to run on.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">KV Cache Growth: Transformer vs LFM</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the slider to change context length</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="margin-bottom:.75rem;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <label style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted2);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
        Context length: <strong id="ctx-len-label" style="color:var(--text)">8 192</strong> tokens
      </label>
      <input type="range" id="ctx-slider" min="1" max="128" value="32"
             style="flex:1;min-width:160px;accent-color:var(--purple)">
    </div>
    <canvas id="kv-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)">
        <span style="width:12px;height:3px;background:#e06c75;display:inline-block;border-radius:2px"></span>
        Transformer KV cache (linear)
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)">
        <span style="width:12px;height:3px;background:#10b981;display:inline-block;border-radius:2px"></span>
        LFM recurrent state (constant)
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The green line stays flat because LFMs maintain a <em>fixed-size recurrent state</em> — they compress context
  into a bounded representation instead of storing every past token. When new input arrives, the state is
  <em>updated in place</em>, not extended. This is the architectural bet Liquid AI made from day one.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-origins" data-code="origins">
<div class="section-no">02 — Origins: From LTC to CfC</div>
<h2>Liquid Neural Networks: The Mathematical Lineage</h2>

<p>
  Liquid Foundation Models didn't emerge from nowhere. They trace their roots to a decade of MIT research
  on <strong>continuous-time recurrent neural networks</strong> — neural ODEs whose dynamics literally adapt to their inputs.
</p>

<p>
  The progression moves from biological inspiration through ODE solvers to the efficient closed-form that
  makes foundation model training feasible. Step through each generation below:
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="evo-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Standard RNN — static dynamics</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Hidden state updates via a fixed nonlinear mapping. Time constants are learned parameters, fixed at inference. The network cannot adapt its memory timescale to input speed or complexity.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h(t+1) = tanh(W_h · h(t) + W_u · u(t) + b)
τ = fixed parameter    # same for every input</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">LTC Networks (2020) — input-varying time constant</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The hidden state follows a continuous-time ODE. The effective decay rate τ(t) changes dynamically with the current input — fast inputs drive rapid state change; slow or absent inputs allow state to persist. Outputs require an ODE solver at every step.</div>
      <div class="step-code">dh/dt = -[1/τ + f(h,I)] · h + g(h,I) · I
# τ is now a function of (h, I) — it's "liquid"
# requires numerical ODE integration at runtime</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">CfC Networks (2022) — closed-form, no ODE solver</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Hasani et al. derived a closed-form approximation of the LTC ODE by solving the formerly intractable integral analytically. Time t enters <em>explicitly</em> as a parameter — no iterative solver needed. Training/inference is up to 10⁵× faster than the ODE version.</div>
      <div class="step-code">x(t) = σ(f(x,I)) · exp(-t · g(x,I)) + σ(h(x,I))
# f, g, h are learned networks (small MLPs)
# t is explicit — evaluated in one forward pass
# Nature Machine Intelligence 4, 992–1003 (2022)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">LFM Design Space (2024) — generalised operators</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Liquid AI abstracted CfC into a broader family: any "adaptive linear operator whose action is determined by inputs." This unifies attention, SSMs (S4, Mamba), and CfC into a single design space searchable along three axes: token-mixing, channel-mixing, and featurization.</div>
      <div class="step-code">y = A(x) · x        # where A(x) is input-dependent
# attention:  A(x) = softmax(QK^T/√d)
# CfC/SSM:    A(x) = diag(exp(-t · g(x)))
# gated conv: A(x) = Convk, gated by B(x), C(x)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="evo-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="evo-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="evo-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<p>
  The CfC insight is fundamental: by expressing the LTC update as a <em>closed-form function of elapsed time t</em>,
  you get the expressive dynamics of liquid neural networks at the cost of a single feed-forward pass.
  This is what made scaling to foundation model territory tractable.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-designspace" data-code="designspace">
<div class="section-no">03 — LFM Design Space</div>
<h2>Three Structural Dimensions</h2>

<p>
  LFM1 (released September 2024) defined a design space that subsumes transformers, SSMs, and liquid networks
  under a single framework. Every LFM block sits at a point in three-dimensional space:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Token-mixing structure</strong> — how sequence positions interact (global via attention, recurrent via SSM/CfC, local via convolution)</li>
  <li><strong>Channel-mixing structure</strong> — how feature dimensions are combined (MLP, gated MLP, cross-channel operations)</li>
  <li><strong>Featurization</strong> — how much the block's computation adapts to the current input context (language needs heavy featurization; audio/time-series can use lighter modulation)</li>
</ul>

<div class="arch-svg-wrap">
