<p>
  The key insight from Liquid AI's architecture studies: different modalities benefit from different featurization levels.
  Language processing benefits from heavy input-dependent modulation because semantic meaning varies wildly between tokens.
  Time-series and audio have more regular structure and need less computational adaptation per step.
</p>

<p>
  The LFM1 models (1B, 3B, 40B) explored this design space and found operators that consistently outperformed
  transformers of the same size on language benchmarks — "the first time a non-GPT architecture significantly
  outperformed transformer-based models" at scale, according to Liquid AI.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-lfm2arch" data-code="lfm2arch">
<div class="section-no">04 — LFM2 Architecture</div>
<h2>Hardware-in-the-Loop Hybrid Architecture</h2>

<p>
  LFM2 (November 2025) represents a key pivot: instead of theoretically motivated architecture choices,
  the team ran a <strong>hardware-in-the-loop search</strong> that directly optimized for downstream quality
  <em>and</em> on-device latency simultaneously. The search space covered SSMs (S4, Mamba, Mamba2, CfC),
  linear attention variants, and convolution blocks, and evaluated each candidate on actual mobile CPU hardware.
</p>

<p>
  The surprising outcome: when measured on real devices under real latency budgets, the winning architecture
  was much simpler than expected — <strong>gated short convolutions</strong> for most layers, with just a handful
  of standard GQA attention blocks:
</p>

<div class="arch-svg-wrap">
<svg width="600" height="400" viewBox="0 0 600 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
