</div>

<p>
  The gated short convolution block is elegantly simple. Given hidden sequence <strong>h ∈ ℝ^(L×d)</strong>:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Gated Short Convolution</div>
  (<span class="math-var">B</span>, <span class="math-var">C</span>, <span class="math-var">h̃</span>) = Linear(<span class="math-var">h</span>)
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— expand to 3d features, split into 3 equal parts</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">y</span> = <span class="math-var">B</span> ⊙ <span class="math-var">h̃</span>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— input-dependent gate B modulates signal h̃</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">z</span> = Conv<sub>k</sub>(<span class="math-var">y</span>)
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— depthwise 1D convolution (k=3), local context</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">o</span> = Linear<sub>out</sub>(<span class="math-var">C</span> ⊙ <span class="math-var">z</span>)
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">— output gate C applied, projected back to d</span>
</div>

<p>
  This block is cheap: the convolution is depthwise (no cross-channel mixing) with a tiny kernel (k=3).
  No attention weights are computed. No KV cache is needed. The only memory overhead is 3 tokens of local state.
  Given that a handful of GQA blocks handle the long-range retrieval tasks that pure-conv models struggle with,
  this minimal hybrid delivers the best quality-per-latency-per-memory trade-off on CPUs.
</p>

