<h3>What LFM2 Enables</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>LFM2-VL</strong> — vision-language model with SigLIP2 encoder; tunable accuracy-latency via token-efficient visual processing</li>
  <li><strong>LFM2-Audio</strong> — end-to-end audio model (speech-in, text/speech-out) competitive with models 3× larger</li>
  <li><strong>LFM2-ColBERT</strong> — multilingual late-interaction retrieval encoder for RAG pipelines</li>
  <li><strong>LFM2-Nanos</strong> — domain-specific nano models for tool calling, math, data extraction, and RAG</li>
  <li>All released open-weight with ExecuTorch, llama.cpp, and vLLM deployment packages</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content (hidden summary played during audio playback) -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Liquid Foundation Models</h2>
    <p>Welcome to this audio summary of Liquid Foundation Models, or LFMs, an alternative model architecture designed to challenge the dominance of Transformers.</p>
    <p>Transformers rely on the attention mechanism, which suffers from quadratic scaling and massive memory footprints. LFMs replace attention entirely with a recurrent, continuous-time formulation based on Gated State Space Models and Closed-form Continuous-time Neural Networks, or CfCs.</p>
    <p>Because they are recurrent, LFMs process sequences with a constant memory footprint, scaling linearly with input length. This allows them to handle large contexts, like thirty-two thousand tokens, with high efficiency on resource-constrained devices.</p>
    <p>LFMs combine gated short convolutions, state-space formulations, and linear mixing layers. On edge hardware like mobile CPUs, they run up to two times faster than equivalent Transformers. Additionally, at small scales, they show impressive quality: the LFM-one billion parameter model outperforms Llama three point two's one point two billion model by thirteen points on MMLU and twenty-two points on GSM eight K.</p>
    <p>LFMs represent a major step toward non-attention foundation models, showing that recurrent models can match or exceed Transformer quality while operating with linear complexity.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：液态基础模型（LFM）— 超越 Transformer</h2>
    <p>欢迎收听本期语音摘要，今天我们来剖析“液态基础模型”（Liquid Foundation Models，简称 LFM）。这是一种旨在挑战 Transformer 主导地位的新型模型架构。</p>
    <p>传统 Transformer 严重依赖注意力机制，这带来了二次方计算复杂度和巨大的内存占用。LFM 则完全摒弃了注意力机制，采用基于“门控状态空间模型”（Gated State Space Models）和“闭式连续时间神经网络”（CfC）的循环、连续时间公式。</p>
    <p>得益于循环神经网络的设计，LFM 在处理序列时仅消耗恒定的内存，其计算复杂度随输入长度呈线性增长。这使得它们能够在显存受限的设备上高效处理多达三万二千个 Token 的超长上下文。</p>
    <p>在底层，LFM 融合了门控短卷积、状态空间表征和线性混合层。在边缘设备（如手机 CPU）上运行速度比同等规模的 Transformer 快两倍。而在小模型尺度上，LFM 也展现出了极高的知识密度：LFM-1B 参数模型在 MMLU 和 GSM8K 测试中，分别超越了 Llama 3.2 的 1.2B 模型十三分和二十二分。</p>
    <p>液态基础模型证明了无注意力机制的循环神经网络在小尺度上完全能与 Transformer 媲美甚至超越，为高效率的基础模型研发开辟了新道路。</p>
  </div>
</div>

